{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788368528,"nonce":"agOeBNn59Lg7WfSQMax/3Tzfd1A=","title":"Observed live on the bench, 2026-09-02, while proving `eef837a`. A fresh `Corpse_Cat` was unforbidden, the butcher bill went `workable`, the run advanced ~80,000 ticks — and the corpse was **gone**, with no `Meat_Cat` produced and no butcher job ever run. The bill went back to `asleep-no-matching-ingredient` with `scanned: 2`.","message":"A wild animal ate it. That is ordinary RimWorld. But from the protocol's side the thing simply ceased to exist between two calls, with nothing anywhere saying so.\n\nEvan's framing, which sets the scope: *\"all that matters from this is that the corpse got disposed of and can't be eaten anymore, so simplicity over fixing every case, the agent just has to not panic and think the game broke.\"*\n\n\n`Verse/HediffSet.GetMissingPartsCommonAncestors()` on the corpse's inner pawn. A predator eats a corpse progressively, so a partially-eaten corpse carries missing parts; fire produces the same shape faster. A corpse at 60% missing is visibly being disposed of, and the agent can stop planning to butcher it.\n\nThe mod currently journals nothing when a thing is destroyed — `JournalHooks.cs` covers construction frames only.\n\n\n1. A corpse row in `things --detail` (and in `bills`' `rejected_sample`, which already carries `rot_stage`) publishes a **missing-part fraction** — one number, derived from `GetMissingPartsCommonAncestors` against the body's part count. Cite the member.\n2. That is the whole feature. **Do NOT** attempt to attribute a cause, identify the predator, distinguish fire from teeth, or track a corpse's history. Those are the cases this issue is explicitly declining to fix.\n3. `checklists/turn.md`'s production read gains one line: a corpse whose missing-part fraction is climbing is being disposed of, not waiting for you — do not keep planning a bill around it.\n4. Bench check (markdown, not a Python suite): read a fresh corpse's fraction, let a predator work on it, read it again, assert the number moved.\n\n\n`eef837a` killed three colonists because the agent could not see why a bill would not run. This is the same class one step further out: the agent now gets a correct `no-matching-ingredient` verdict but cannot tell \"your ingredient was eaten\" from \"the game is behaving strangely.\" The cost of the wrong conclusion is a run that stops trusting its own instruments.\n\nRelated: `f1a1700` (parked) is the room-and-building half of \"things vanish silently\". This is the thing half, and it is much cheaper because a corpse has a readable state rather than needing a durable identity.","files":null}]}